## 1. The Core Formulas and Symbolic Syntax
To audit how an application handles object references and boundaries, you map user entities, privileges, and server objects to mathematical sets.
## Set Operations and Privilege Boundaries

* Intersection ($A \cap B$): Elements belonging to both set A and set B. Used to define intersecting permissions or common roles.
* Union ($A \cup B$): All elements in set A, set B, or both. Used to calculate total cumulative privileges.
* Set Difference ($A \setminus B$ or A - B): Elements in set A that are not in set B. Used to define isolated administration scopes.
* Cartesian Product (A × B): All possible pairs (a, b) where a ∈ A and b ∈ B. Used to audit matrix access controls (every user crossed against every permission asset).
* Power Set ($\mathcal{P}(A)$): The set of all subsets of A. If a site has n permissions, there are exactly $2^n$ unique permission profiles possible.

## Lattices and Hasse Diagrams (Access Control Architecture)
Access hierarchies are modeled as a Partially Ordered Set (Poset) under a relation ≤ (denoting "has equal or less privilege than"). A securely designed hierarchy forms a Lattice, meaning any two security states have a unique Lowest Upper Bound (LUB) and Greatest Lower Bound (GLB).
