## 2. Step-by-Step System Testing Using Set Boundaries
Let’s apply Set Theory to audit an Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR) or an unauthenticated information exposure flaw outside an account.
The Scenario: A betting site serves casino invoices. The developer wants to isolate public tournament files from private individual invoices. They define two classifications:

* Set P: The set of all Public Objects available to unauthenticated visitors.
* Set V: The set of all Private Vault Objects (like personal transaction statements).

The developer implements a routing handler that takes an incoming object requested by an unauthenticated visitor (x) and attempts to ensure it belongs strictly to the public boundary. They write this code logic:

// Check if the requested file ID exists in the global public downloads catalog
if (in_array($x, $public_catalog) || !in_array($x, $blacklisted_admin_files)) {
    serve_file($x);
}

Let's model this using Set Theory and De Morgan's Laws to see why this logic exposes private data.

* Let P = public_catalog
* Let B = blacklisted_admin_files (a tiny subset of critical configuration files)
* The developer allows access if: $x \in P \lor x \notin B$

## Mathematical Boundary Breakdown
We want to evaluate what happens to an object x that belongs to a regular user's private invoice pool (V), where private invoices are neither public nor explicitly hardcoded in the admin blacklist.
$$\text{Condition: } x \in V \implies (x \notin P) \land (x \notin B)$$ 
Now substitute these values into the developer's allowance equation:
$$(x \in P) \lor (x \notin B) \implies (\text{False}) \lor (\text{True}) \implies \text{True}$$ 
## The Structural Failure
Because the developer used a logical disjunction ($\lor$) connected to a loose negative set inclusion (x ∉ B), the boundary leaks. Any private invoice ID or random file that sits outside the public set—but is simply missing from the admin blacklist—will evaluate to True and be served to an unauthenticated crawler.
To fix this mathematically, the boundary constraint must switch from an open set structure to a strict intersection requirement where an asset can only be served if it explicitly belongs to the authorized public set:
$$\text{Secure Condition: } \text{Access} = \{x \mid x \in P\}$$ 
